et by 800.\n* On Round 2, you performed action: 0.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold **King of Spades, 7 of Clubs**.  \nThe board is: **4♦, K♥, 7♦, 3♥, 10♣** — all face-up.  \n\nThis gives you:  \n- **Two Pair: Kings and Sevens**, with kicker **10♣** (from the board).  \nYour hand: K♠, 7♣ → pairs with K♥ and 7♦ → making two pair.  \nNo possible straight or flush for you. The board has no three of the same suit (hearts has K, 3, 10 — only three, but you have no heart to complete a flush), so flush is unlikely.  \nStraight possibilities? The highest possible straight on this board would be Q-J-T-9-8 or J-T-9-8-7 — not completed. You don’t have connectors to make it. So straight is out.  \n\nYou currently have a **strong medium-strength hand**: Two Pair (Kings and Sevens). However, the board is relatively dry but not without danger. An opponent could have:\n- A higher two pair (e.g., 10 and K, or 10 and J if they held T-J).\n- A set (like 4-4, 3-3, or 10-10).\n- Or even a straight if they had J-9 (though unlikely given betting history).\n\nBut crucially, your **opponent raised heavily on the turn (Round 2)** — first calling your 100, then raising 200, then after your re-raise to 400, they went 800. You called. This suggests **strength** — possibly a better two pair, a set, or even a made straight.\n\nHowever, you **checked** after calling their 800, which may signal weakness. Now it's the river — last card already out — and **you're first to act again**.\n\nYour options:  \n- **Fold (-1)**: Only if you think you’re beat almost certainly. But two pair is hard to fold here without reads of extreme strength.\n- **Check (0)**: Safe. Let them bluff or value bet. If they have a worse hand, they might bet anyway. If they have a better hand, you avoid putting more in.\n- **Raise (e.g., 100 or more)**: Could be a bluff or value bet. But do you look strong? Your prior check may suggest weakness, so a small raise might induce a fold from weaker pairs (e.g., top pair K-x or T-x). But likely, only worse hands fold, and better hands call — so poor value.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill unless you suspect they’ll fold a decent hand. With ~18,800 chips, going all-in is ~18,800. Already 1,500+ in pot (exact pot size not given, but at least 1,600 based on bets). Risky.\n\nGiven opponent’s aggressive re-raises, they likely have:\n- Set of Tens, Threes, Fours\n- Two pair like 10-3, 10-4, or even K-10\n- Possibly K-K or 7-7 (less likely)\n\nYou beat K-x, 7-x, or weak two pair like 4-3, but lose to sets or stronger kickers in two pair.\n\nBut importantly: **you checked on the river after being raised twice**. That typically signals hesitation. A sudden raise now looks like a bluff or thin value. But few worse hands will call — most players fold worse two pair or top pair to river raises.\n\nBest play: **Check and induce a bluff**. Since you acted weakly before, opponent may bet with air or marginal hands (e.g., A-K, K-Q, thinking they have best hand). Or, if they have a strong hand, they may value bet.\n\nSo checking allows you to **call a small bet** or **fold if they shove big**.\n\nReason:  \nChecking keeps the pot manageable and exploits opponent’s tendency to bet the river (given their aggression). It avoids overcommitting with a vulnerable but decent hand. Raising risks chasing away worse hands and getting called by better ones. Folding is too weak — two pair is still credible.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
